.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-init - Guided first-run setup: environment, key, sign-in


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 init [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Walk through the setup a first payment needs, in one command:
.IP "  1." 5
choose an environment (writes base-url, so every later command uses it)
.IP "  2." 5
check the gateway answers
.IP "  3." 5
optionally save a signing key in the OS keychain (referenced later as @name)
.IP "  4." 5
optionally sign in with it (SIWE), caching a session for that gateway

.PP
Each step can be skipped, so it is safe to re-run on a machine that is already
half-configured. Nothing here is irreversible: the same steps are 'rail0 env',
\&'rail0 health', 'rail0 keys add' and 'rail0 auth login', and the setup can be
undone with 'rail0 keys rm' / 'rail0 auth logout'.

.PP
Interactive by design — it asks questions. In a script, run those four commands
directly instead.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for init


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 init
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0(1)\fP
